<section aria-labelledby="elf-startup">
  <h2 id="elf-startup" class="prm-section-heading ruled">ELF startup and memory in pTOS</h2>
  <p>Use <a href="/reference/pexec/">Pexec(0)</a> to load and run an ELF executable.
    The Pexec reference gives the conditions for asynchronous launch and process-image replacement.
    On 68k builds with public staged PRG loading, <code>Pexec(3)</code> refuses an ELF file with
    <code>EPLFMT</code>. MMU ARM and x32 refuse mode 3 with <code>EINVFN</code>.</p>
  <table class="prm-rule-table">
    <caption>ELF image placement and startup</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Launch path</th><th scope="col">Memory placement</th><th scope="col">Initial entry</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Static ELF, single address space</th>
        <td>Load segments into the TPA following the basepage, with padding where alignment requires it.
          Startup code can release surplus TPA with <a href="/historical/en/mshrink/">Mshrink</a>
          after reserving the image, argument storage and stack.</td>
        <td>Adjusted <code>e_entry</code> with the target’s basepage convention.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Static ELF, private address space</th>
        <td>Load segments directly into the process’s private pages. The TPA holds the basepage. The image,
          startup storage and user stack occupy their own ranges.</td>
        <td>Adjusted <code>e_entry</code> with the target’s basepage convention and a private user stack.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ELF with <code>PT_INTERP</code></th>
        <td>Map the executable, interpreter and initial stack into the child’s private address space.
          The interpreter resolves shared-library dependencies before it transfers control to the executable.</td>
        <td>The interpreter starts with its stack pointer at <code>argc</code>, followed by <code>argv</code>,
          <code>envp</code> and the auxiliary vector. See <a href={elfEntryPath}>ELF32 process entry</a>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <table class="prm-rule-table">
    <caption>Basepage at ordinary static ELF entry</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Target</th><th scope="col">Basepage location</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">68k</th><td><code>4(SP)</code>, with <code>A0</code> cleared.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ARM</th><td><code>R0</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">x32</th><td><code>RDI</code>, with <code>RSI = 0</code> for an ordinary program.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For static ELF, <code>p_tbase</code> holds the relocated entry address and <code>p_dlen</code> is zero.
    The ELF loader reports the file-backed image through <code>p_tlen</code> and the trailing region through
    <code>p_bbase</code> and <code>p_blen</code>. Use the <code>PT_LOAD</code> records for individual segment boundaries.</p>
  <p>The classic TPA shrink example below applies to a 68k PRG. If its startup code reserves the stack in BSS,
    <code>p_blen</code> already includes those bytes.
    See also <a href="/subsystems/gemdos/the-program-format/#elf-executables">The program format</a>.</p>
</section>
